xnu 0509: a device node for the HAL, and killname

MondrianAudio.driver lives inside mediaserverd, which is sandboxed: the three
ports whose audio reaches iOS (ios-redfin, pixel3-ios, ios-guacamole) all give
it a character device, not a sysctl. This adds `/dev/mondrian-audio` as the
second end of the ring 0507 already has, so the service, the graph and the
amplifiers are untouched.

The ABI is the plug-in's (tools/mondrian-hal/mondrian_audio.h): S16LE stereo at
48000 frames a second, whole frames, never blocking; what does not fit is
dropped and counted. `write()` converts to the 32-bit slots the PCM converter
expects. `ioctl` takes the volume control (iOS keeps its speaker in software
volume and sets it once to 0 dB, so the kernel stays at unity and only the mute
is honoured) and the counters the plug-in logs. The sysctl channel stays as the
diagnostic path.

`killname NAME` signals every process with that name. `kill` takes a PID and
`kill mediaserverd` is `kill 0`, which answers `kill 0 -> 3` and kills nothing
— measured 2026-11-18 on SpringBoard and again today on mediaserverd, where the
plug-in was installed and never loaded. launchd starts the daemon again at
once, which is what makes the HAL loader read the directory.

The plug-in also needs two things outside the kernel, both taken from the
ports that work: the device UIDs `Speaker` and `Hawking` (VirtualAudio keeps
everything on Null_Device without the companion) and `/product/haptics` with
`closed-loop` in the device tree for iOS's haptics later.
